1
00:00:11,653 --> 00:00:20,158
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this is from the Anguttara Nikaya, from the book of the Fuyis, the discourse with the Kālamās of Kesamutta.

2
00:00:21,639 --> 00:00:22,320
[SPEAKER_02]: So have I heard.

3
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:31,746
[SPEAKER_02]: At one time, the Buddha was wandering in the land of the coastal lands together with a large saṅgha of bhikkhus when he arrived at a town of the Kālamās named Kesamutta.

4
00:00:33,075 --> 00:00:42,282
[SPEAKER_02]: The Kalamās of Keśā-Muṭhā heard, it seems the ascetic Gautama, the Sakyaṃ, gone forth from a Sakyaṃ family, has arrived at Keśā-Muṭhā.

5
00:00:42,682 --> 00:00:44,003
[SPEAKER_02]: He has this good reputation.

6
00:00:44,664 --> 00:00:48,627
[SPEAKER_02]: That blessed one is an Arahant, fully awakened Buddha, and so on.

7
00:00:48,987 --> 00:00:52,090
[SPEAKER_02]: And then they come to the conclusion, it is good to see such Arahants.

8
00:00:53,197 --> 00:00:54,920
[SPEAKER_02]: Then the Gālamās went up to the Buddha.

9
00:00:55,280 --> 00:01:06,116
[SPEAKER_02]: Before sitting down to one side, some bowed, some exchanged pleasantries, some held up their joined palms towards the Buddha, some announced their name and clan, while some kept silent.

10
00:01:07,827 --> 00:01:15,251
[SPEAKER_02]: Seated to one side, the Kalama said to the Buddha, Bhante, there are some ascetics and brahmins who come to Kesamutta.

11
00:01:15,731 --> 00:01:23,175
[SPEAKER_02]: They explain and promote only their own doctrine, while they attack, deride, disparage and smear the doctrines of others.

12
00:01:23,996 --> 00:01:26,937
[SPEAKER_02]: Then some other ascetics and brahmins come to Kesamutta.

13
00:01:27,298 --> 00:01:35,082
[SPEAKER_02]: They too explain and promote only their own doctrine, while they attack, deride, disparage and smear the doctrines of others.

14
00:01:36,211 --> 00:01:39,293
[SPEAKER_02]: So, Bhante, we're doubting and uncertain.

15
00:01:40,314 --> 00:01:46,919
[SPEAKER_02]: I wonder who of these respected ascetics and brahmins speak the truth, and who speaks falsehood?

16
00:01:48,901 --> 00:01:54,025
[SPEAKER_02]: Then the Buddha replies, it is appropriate, Kalamāsa, for you to be doubting and uncertain.

17
00:01:54,786 --> 00:01:57,548
[SPEAKER_02]: Doubt has come up in you about an uncertain matter.

18
00:01:59,222 --> 00:02:18,104
[SPEAKER_02]: Kalamās don't go by oral transmission, by lineage, by hearsay, by canonical authority, by logic, by inference, by analysis of properties, by the acceptance of review after consideration, by the appearance of competence, or by the thought, the ascetic is our respected teacher.

19
00:02:19,070 --> 00:02:30,554
[SPEAKER_02]: When you know for yourselves, these things are detrimental, blameworthy, criticized by sensible people, and when undertaken, they lead to harm and suffering, then you should give them up.

20
00:02:32,034 --> 00:02:35,595
[SPEAKER_02]: What do you think, Kalamās, when greed arises in a person?

21
00:02:36,135 --> 00:02:38,156
[SPEAKER_02]: Does it do so for their welfare or harm?

22
00:02:39,036 --> 00:02:41,097
[SPEAKER_02]: And then the Kalamās reply, harm, Bhante.

23
00:02:41,908 --> 00:02:51,111
[SPEAKER_02]: A greedy individual, overcome by greed, kills living creatures, steals, commits adultery, lies, and encourages others to do the same.

24
00:02:51,752 --> 00:02:53,592
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that for their lasting harm and suffering?

25
00:02:54,292 --> 00:02:54,913
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, Bhante.

26
00:02:55,333 --> 00:02:56,373
[SPEAKER_02]: What do you think, Kalamās?

27
00:02:56,713 --> 00:03:09,718
[SPEAKER_02]: When aversion arises in a person, and then it repeats, and then it also talks about when delusion arises in a person, that's all detrimental and blameworthy.

28
00:03:11,197 --> 00:03:29,726
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, the Buddha says, so, kalamās, when I said, don't go by old transmission, by lineage, by hearsay, by canonical authority, by logic, inference, by analysis of properties, by the acceptance of review after consideration, by the appearance of competence or by the thought the ascetic is our respected teacher.

29
00:03:30,702 --> 00:03:40,577
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, when you know for yourselves these things are detrimental, blameworthy, criticized by sensible people, and when undertaken, they lead to harm and suffering, then you should give them up.

30
00:03:41,078 --> 00:03:43,362
[SPEAKER_02]: That is what I said, and that is why I said it.

31
00:03:44,708 --> 00:03:48,452
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, it continues in the opposite way.

32
00:03:48,993 --> 00:04:05,010
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when you know non-greed arises in a person, non-aversion arises in a person, and non-delusion or middle-mindedness arises in a person, then you should know for yourself that that's beneficial and not blameworthy.

33
00:04:07,282 --> 00:04:15,445
[SPEAKER_02]: Then the sutta continues and says, then that noble disciple is rid of longing, rid of ill will, unmuddled, aware and recollected.

34
00:04:16,025 --> 00:04:22,808
[SPEAKER_02]: He abides having pervaded with a mind of friendliness the first direction, likewise second, third and fourth.

35
00:04:23,528 --> 00:04:28,053
[SPEAKER_01]: These are the Brahmaviharas.

36
00:04:28,073 --> 00:04:28,253
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

37
00:04:28,314 --> 00:04:40,247
[SPEAKER_02]: In the same way above, below, across, everywhere, he abides having pervaded the whole world with a mind of friendliness, abundant, expansive, immeasurable, free of enmity and ill-will.

38
00:04:40,787 --> 00:04:43,590
[SPEAKER_02]: And so it goes on for the other three Brahmaviharas.

39
00:04:45,212 --> 00:04:54,397
[SPEAKER_03]: So, this is one of the more well-known discourses, the discourses to the Kalamās, and there's quite a few things to unpack here.

40
00:04:55,037 --> 00:05:01,520
[SPEAKER_03]: So, perhaps you can start with the very first thing that the Buddha says, like, don't go by tradition, right?

41
00:05:01,640 --> 00:05:09,584
[SPEAKER_03]: Oral transmission, logical inference, or, you know, accepting a view after pondering it, and so on.

42
00:05:10,224 --> 00:05:12,365
[SPEAKER_03]: If you don't do any of that,

43
00:05:14,415 --> 00:05:15,077
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, what do you do?

44
00:05:16,300 --> 00:05:19,630
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, how do you know that you're practicing rightly?

45
00:05:20,352 --> 00:05:21,234
[SPEAKER_03]: What do people here say?

46
00:05:35,941 --> 00:05:42,387
[SPEAKER_00]: My first thought is that he's not necessarily dismissing these things as tools.

47
00:05:43,047 --> 00:05:48,112
[SPEAKER_00]: In the sense, like, he's not saying, oh, you know, don't go by logic.

48
00:05:49,153 --> 00:05:58,515
[SPEAKER_00]: A false conclusion to draw from that would be like, okay, then that means the dhamma doesn't have to be logical, or like things don't have to make sense.

49
00:05:59,756 --> 00:06:09,378
[SPEAKER_00]: But what I get from that is more, don't fall into a complacency of like, okay, just because you've made this logical analysis of

50
00:06:10,278 --> 00:06:16,082
[SPEAKER_00]: If you put every sutta into a box, you can draw all the parallels, it all makes perfect sense, no contradictions.

51
00:06:18,263 --> 00:06:24,067
[SPEAKER_00]: Not to... like, if that's kind of your baseline, then you're going to be missing the mark still.

52
00:06:25,208 --> 00:06:26,769
[SPEAKER_03]: And why?

53
00:06:27,530 --> 00:06:35,295
[SPEAKER_03]: What is it that all those things have in common, but that still makes it not dhamma?

54
00:06:36,496 --> 00:06:39,818
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not personal, I'd say.

55
00:06:41,398 --> 00:06:41,979
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not personal.

56
00:06:42,840 --> 00:06:45,802
[SPEAKER_03]: But some people can be very personal about accepting a teacher, for example.

57
00:06:46,663 --> 00:06:46,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

58
00:06:48,805 --> 00:07:00,077
[SPEAKER_03]: Or, after having spent like 20-30 years pondering a view, coming to the conclusion this must be right, the Buddha comes along and says, well, the fact that you've pondered it for a long time doesn't necessarily mean it's correct.

59
00:07:02,539 --> 00:07:05,502
[SPEAKER_03]: So, can there be a... is there a more accurate factor?

60
00:07:11,264 --> 00:07:35,990
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm trying to think of something to say that's not just kind of what he followed up with, but since you, like... Is it on that personal level where, even if that... the teacher dies, the beloved teacher, and you forget everything they ever said, can you still

61
00:07:38,402 --> 00:07:39,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you still free?

62
00:07:39,763 --> 00:07:43,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, like the Buddha says, do you still know right from wrong?

63
00:07:44,348 --> 00:07:45,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you still know liberation?

64
00:07:55,900 --> 00:07:56,641
[SPEAKER_01]: The thing there is...

65
00:07:58,987 --> 00:08:02,028
[SPEAKER_01]: The funny thing with the sutta is that the second part is usually left out.

66
00:08:03,048 --> 00:08:07,589
[SPEAKER_01]: It's usually just like, okay, don't go by tradition, don't go by hearsay, don't go by logic.

67
00:08:08,330 --> 00:08:18,953
[SPEAKER_01]: And people quote that as a kind of a testament to how Buddhism is different from other religions, where it's like your own experience is the measure or something.

68
00:08:20,153 --> 00:08:25,436
[SPEAKER_01]: The thing is that carries the implication that it's almost like, well, like make your own truth or something like that.

69
00:08:25,456 --> 00:08:32,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Like there's some individual truth and you're supposed to pick what resonates with you and remove the rest or something.

70
00:08:32,841 --> 00:08:40,705
[SPEAKER_01]: But it actually says there, what is it that you... it's implied though, but it says, what is it that you're supposed to use as a standard?

71
00:08:40,806 --> 00:08:44,188
[SPEAKER_01]: And it says, when greed, aversion and delusion arises.

72
00:08:45,108 --> 00:08:46,489
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh...

73
00:08:48,068 --> 00:09:00,762
[SPEAKER_01]: What you said just now, of keeping it personal, the thing is, like, that that's exactly what it is, but you don't do it just by... Because you can be personal, but objective.

74
00:09:01,703 --> 00:09:02,344
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the problem.

75
00:09:02,364 --> 00:09:07,469
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is, like, all these permutations of attitudes,

76
00:09:08,370 --> 00:09:17,592
[SPEAKER_01]: The issue with them is, you think that something objective, something external, something that's public, is what determines right and wrong.

77
00:09:17,612 --> 00:09:20,533
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that's what all those things, to a degree, have in common.

78
00:09:20,553 --> 00:09:20,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

79
00:09:20,893 --> 00:09:21,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

80
00:09:21,173 --> 00:09:30,275
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if it's, as it says, your own logic and reasoning, it's something objective, that means like, okay, every day I do this,

81
00:09:31,495 --> 00:09:35,656
[SPEAKER_01]: It's right because, logically, I have made all the right connections.

82
00:09:35,736 --> 00:09:37,116
[SPEAKER_01]: It makes sense logically.

83
00:09:37,836 --> 00:09:49,399
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you do something with a mind of greed, even if it makes sense, even if it's what the teacher said, even if it's what tradition said, it's wrong because it's done with a mind of greed.

84
00:09:50,359 --> 00:09:57,441
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, the... what is rooted in greed, aversion and delusion, that is the truth.

85
00:09:57,741 --> 00:09:58,661
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not a...

86
00:09:59,581 --> 00:10:04,425
[SPEAKER_01]: So, presence of greed, aversion, and delusion, and absence of greed, aversion, and delusion, that's the truth.

87
00:10:04,645 --> 00:10:05,986
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the criteria.

88
00:10:06,006 --> 00:10:14,232
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's like... that's not debatable, in the sense that you're either acting with those things or not.

89
00:10:15,173 --> 00:10:15,893
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the...

90
00:10:18,941 --> 00:10:24,886
[SPEAKER_01]: What I'm saying is, it's not saying that there are different truths or something like that, that you need to find your own.

91
00:10:25,607 --> 00:10:30,491
[SPEAKER_01]: It's saying, that's the only defining criteria for... Yeah.

92
00:10:30,751 --> 00:10:32,973
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that is universal, but not objective.

93
00:10:33,294 --> 00:10:34,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

94
00:10:34,194 --> 00:10:39,519
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's because, not just objective in the terms of individual people, but in terms of yourself as well.

95
00:10:39,619 --> 00:10:41,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, one morning, you can be having

96
00:10:43,142 --> 00:10:46,884
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be doing something that's seemingly good, like helping someone, let's say.

97
00:10:47,044 --> 00:10:52,147
[SPEAKER_01]: And in the morning, it's rooted in a mind of non-greed, non-aversion.

98
00:10:52,387 --> 00:10:54,628
[SPEAKER_01]: Out of a genuine intention, you were just helping this person.

99
00:10:54,668 --> 00:10:56,089
[SPEAKER_01]: But in the evening, you're doing it again.

100
00:10:56,629 --> 00:10:59,551
[SPEAKER_01]: And now, it's rooted in just desire for company and distraction.

101
00:11:00,671 --> 00:11:05,614
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like an essential desire or whatever.

102
00:11:06,254 --> 00:11:10,276
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you think that, okay, the Buddha said that helping people is good.

103
00:11:10,456 --> 00:11:14,598
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha said that, you know, it makes sense that it's that it's wholesome and whatever.

104
00:11:14,638 --> 00:11:21,861
[SPEAKER_01]: And you think of it about it in that objective sense, you would overlook that actually was defining the action is what state of mind you have right now.

105
00:11:22,102 --> 00:11:22,342
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

106
00:11:26,584 --> 00:11:47,494
[SPEAKER_01]: The actions that it says there... Well, the precepts in themselves, they're... Like, if it were possible to kill without a mind of aversion, to steal without a mind of aversion, to lie without a mind of aversion... Or a mind of greed or delusion, for that matter.

107
00:11:47,774 --> 00:11:48,434
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

108
00:11:49,034 --> 00:11:51,976
[SPEAKER_01]: Or any kind of unwholesome mental state, then these things wouldn't be wrong.

109
00:11:52,796 --> 00:11:54,297
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter what the tradition says.

110
00:11:54,858 --> 00:12:00,963
[SPEAKER_01]: What matters is, these things are wrong, because they always carry those mental states.

111
00:12:01,163 --> 00:12:14,733
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from that point, if somebody is following the precepts, just because the tradition says that you need to follow the precepts, because that's what Buddhists do, that means that they haven't understood.

112
00:12:16,855 --> 00:12:27,340
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not trying to understand the principle of what makes those things wholesome and what makes the abstinence from those things wholesome.

113
00:12:28,521 --> 00:12:36,125
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the fact that, to that extent, you'd have a mindset of less greed, less aversion and less delusion.

114
00:12:36,165 --> 00:12:39,127
[SPEAKER_01]: You take that as your standard and then you take that principle further.

115
00:12:39,227 --> 00:12:40,688
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you just stay on that

116
00:12:42,149 --> 00:12:43,050
[SPEAKER_01]: That happens often.

117
00:12:43,090 --> 00:12:55,687
[SPEAKER_01]: People with meditation and stuff, especially, there's this tendency to assume that if I just do this, if I just go through these motions every day, every time I do it, it's going to be wholesome.

118
00:12:56,827 --> 00:12:59,090
[SPEAKER_01]: That means you're... because somebody told you, really.

119
00:12:59,190 --> 00:13:03,796
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like somebody told you that if you repeat these things, eventually you will get to enlightenment.

120
00:13:04,196 --> 00:13:09,764
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, again, depending on... depending fully on tradition or hearsay.

121
00:13:09,904 --> 00:13:14,169
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're not seeing what is the mental state that I have presently.

122
00:13:14,189 --> 00:13:14,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

123
00:13:14,990 --> 00:13:17,191
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it a mindset of greed, aversion or delusion?

124
00:13:17,651 --> 00:13:21,312
[SPEAKER_01]: So that I can, if I know that, I can directly cultivate liberation.

125
00:13:21,352 --> 00:13:25,973
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to wait for some miracle to happen after I do this practice for 10 years.

126
00:13:26,333 --> 00:13:28,493
[SPEAKER_03]: So this is with the Vettel of Śrīla Bhāgavata Paramārtha, right?

127
00:13:28,513 --> 00:13:28,613
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

128
00:13:28,653 --> 00:13:30,654
[SPEAKER_03]: There's this external component there.

129
00:13:31,454 --> 00:13:33,235
[SPEAKER_03]: Conformity is often involved.

130
00:13:33,275 --> 00:13:42,077
[SPEAKER_03]: Finding safety in just... even if it's self-imposed, I'm going to do this every time in the morning and in the evening, which then allows you to completely overlook

131
00:13:43,031 --> 00:13:43,251
[SPEAKER_03]: Right?

132
00:13:43,432 --> 00:13:47,296
[SPEAKER_03]: Sign of the mind and where the intentions actually are, which is the factor there.

133
00:13:47,396 --> 00:13:56,407
[SPEAKER_03]: And this almost seems to me like a sort of further elaboration of what would be involved in that factor of Śrīla Bhāgavatamāsa, which prevents one.

134
00:13:56,788 --> 00:14:00,893
[SPEAKER_03]: It's one of the first three factors that prevent one from becoming a noble disciple.

135
00:14:01,575 --> 00:14:09,777
[SPEAKER_03]: Interestingly enough, that sort of continues after the Buddha has listed that, and he says, when this noble disciple knows that his mind is, right?

136
00:14:09,877 --> 00:14:12,378
[SPEAKER_03]: So, and then, it's kind of like, well, hang on, hang on a minute.

137
00:14:12,398 --> 00:14:19,940
[SPEAKER_03]: How did it suddenly go from kalamāsura, confused, and then the Buddha suddenly introducing noble disciple, well, actually, all that's implied.

138
00:14:19,960 --> 00:14:26,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because if you understand, if you fully, actually, the Majjhima Nikaya 9, it defines what is a noble disciple.

139
00:14:26,622 --> 00:14:27,862
[SPEAKER_01]: The very first thing it says, it's

140
00:14:28,482 --> 00:14:34,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Somebody who understands wholesome and unwholesome, and understands the root of the wholesome and the root of the unwholesome.

141
00:14:34,327 --> 00:14:35,168
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is that root?

142
00:14:35,929 --> 00:14:37,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Greed, aversion, and delusion.

143
00:14:37,110 --> 00:14:38,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion.

144
00:14:39,132 --> 00:14:43,355
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you take that principle to the full extent, and you see it starts with the precepts.

145
00:14:43,455 --> 00:14:46,938
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why... that's like the level where everyone will be able to see this.

146
00:14:47,199 --> 00:14:51,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if they have a basic degree of self-honesty, they will see that doing those things,

147
00:14:52,083 --> 00:14:56,225
[SPEAKER_01]: It carries more greed, aversion, and delusion than not doing those things.

148
00:14:56,725 --> 00:15:06,869
[SPEAKER_01]: And with that as a basis, they can start then refining it further, to the point where, okay, verbal actions, and then, finally, mental actions, which is how you would cultivate samādhi in the end.

149
00:15:07,089 --> 00:15:07,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

150
00:15:07,629 --> 00:15:09,150
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

151
00:15:10,510 --> 00:15:15,995
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, so, maybe we can also clear up, like, I mean, in the beginning there will be greed, aversion and delusion.

152
00:15:16,035 --> 00:15:17,036
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the starting point.

153
00:15:17,457 --> 00:15:21,661
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's not black and white, like there's either full-blown greed, hatred and delusion or there's nothing.

154
00:15:21,701 --> 00:15:23,062
[SPEAKER_03]: But, it will sort of...

155
00:15:23,703 --> 00:15:23,923
[SPEAKER_03]: Right?

156
00:15:24,063 --> 00:15:28,447
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you can compare your own experience to a degree and you can see this is less greedy.

157
00:15:29,488 --> 00:15:31,209
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, like I said, it's from this starting point that you are.

158
00:15:31,450 --> 00:15:32,951
[SPEAKER_03]: What mind do I have?

159
00:15:48,411 --> 00:16:03,227
[SPEAKER_01]: You would see for yourself, if I just... somebody who's not keeping the five precepts, let's say, they would see clearly that if they abstain from those things, they will be moving in the opposite direction of greed, aversion and delusion.

160
00:16:04,288 --> 00:16:07,671
[SPEAKER_01]: But if then you just take it as...

161
00:16:09,070 --> 00:16:13,734
[SPEAKER_01]: That's actually why you would be able to skip the development of virtue many times.

162
00:16:14,655 --> 00:16:15,396
[SPEAKER_01]: Wrongly, of course.

163
00:16:16,137 --> 00:16:20,641
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you haven't actually understood, like, why those precepts are wholesome.

164
00:16:21,141 --> 00:16:28,228
[SPEAKER_01]: You just take them conditionally because you think, well, it comes bundled with the whole other aspects of the practice.

165
00:16:28,648 --> 00:16:32,732
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you never actually understood why that foundation was important.

166
00:16:32,792 --> 00:16:33,973
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, it feels like

167
00:16:34,774 --> 00:16:35,595
[SPEAKER_01]: You're progressing.

168
00:16:36,396 --> 00:16:38,800
