[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you start with that, and it says, when the monk is accomplished in virtue, then I tell him to be censor-strained.

343
00:32:53,032 --> 00:32:57,774
[SPEAKER_01]: When the monk is accomplished in censor-strained, then I tell him to be moderate in eating, and so on.

344
00:32:57,814 --> 00:33:03,137
[SPEAKER_01]: Because these are just layers of pre-division and delusion that you're progressively working with.

345
00:33:03,217 --> 00:33:10,100
[SPEAKER_01]: But even just with the basic precepts, if you keep them out of that sense of complacency, because

346
00:33:12,581 --> 00:33:14,623
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, because that's what the other monks are doing or something.

347
00:33:14,643 --> 00:33:15,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Or out of duty.

348
00:33:15,884 --> 00:33:16,784
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, sense of duty.

349
00:33:16,804 --> 00:33:17,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

350
00:33:17,465 --> 00:33:26,952
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense of... or the conditional sense of like, okay, if I just do this, it will help the other stuff that I actually regard as important.

351
00:33:27,613 --> 00:33:32,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you never actually even took up the first thread of understanding greed aversion.

352
00:33:32,516 --> 00:33:32,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

353
00:33:38,158 --> 00:33:40,019
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can review your mental states.

354
00:33:40,259 --> 00:33:41,820
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the practice.

355
00:33:43,401 --> 00:33:54,807
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're restrained, but then, when the desire arises to do these things that you made a determination to give up, is that pleasant or unpleasant?

356
00:33:54,847 --> 00:33:57,069
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that suffering or is it...?

357
00:33:57,429 --> 00:33:58,710
[SPEAKER_01]: You see, you might overlook...

358
00:33:59,789 --> 00:34:09,294
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that, as it says, greed brings harm and suffering, because you usually are able to get what you want quickly enough.

359
00:34:09,734 --> 00:34:14,016
[SPEAKER_01]: But what happens when you just have the greed, but you don't do anything?

360
00:34:14,096 --> 00:34:15,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that pleasant or unpleasant?

361
00:34:16,057 --> 00:34:21,880
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will see that it's quite... it is what suffering is, basically.

362
00:34:21,900 --> 00:34:26,262
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from that point of view, you get to see, like, oh, all this

363
00:34:27,632 --> 00:34:40,761
[SPEAKER_01]: In the past, when I was regarding these actions as pleasant, it's just because the pleasure of the things I was getting was distracting me from the pain of the mental state that was actually driving me to do those things.

364
00:34:41,150 --> 00:34:54,283
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but then there are also sometimes people who say, no, no, just the desire itself is already pleasant, is usually more pleasant, because when you then actually get what you desire, it's always a disappointment, right?

365
00:34:54,543 --> 00:35:00,650
[SPEAKER_03]: So, they then widely see that even if they get what they want, it's initially pleasant, but afterwards it becomes a disappointment.

366
00:35:01,110 --> 00:35:08,675
[SPEAKER_03]: Then they say, therefore, I just stick on the level of the actual desire, so as to avoid the disappointment of actually getting it.

367
00:35:09,076 --> 00:35:12,918
[SPEAKER_03]: Failing to see that even the desire is more unpleasant still.

368
00:35:13,479 --> 00:35:13,979
[SPEAKER_03]: But why?

369
00:35:14,139 --> 00:35:15,660
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it doesn't leave you alone.

370
00:35:15,760 --> 00:35:18,342
[SPEAKER_03]: It's basically pushing you to act out.

371
00:35:19,103 --> 00:35:21,524
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's the same with the ill will and the aversion.

372
00:35:21,624 --> 00:35:24,346
[SPEAKER_03]: It is not just optional, like, oh, you could do this.

373
00:35:24,366 --> 00:35:25,127
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no.

374
00:35:25,307 --> 00:35:30,411
[SPEAKER_03]: It is very much pushing you in that direction, which at the same time, you know, is not wholesome.

375
00:35:30,991 --> 00:35:40,058
[SPEAKER_03]: And so, when the mind is overcome by these unwholesome states, there cannot be peace, because it's forcing you to act, right?

376
00:35:40,358 --> 00:35:46,963
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you think it's not forcing me, well, are you sure that you are fully withdrawn from all the forms of greed aversion?

377
00:35:47,103 --> 00:35:47,723
[SPEAKER_03]: That's then it.

378
00:35:47,743 --> 00:35:58,029
[SPEAKER_03]: So, when there's doubt, well, I don't feel pressured by my mind, well, then people can either refine their precepts or introduce these further steps of the gradual training, such as sensory strains, motivation in eating.

379
00:35:58,049 --> 00:35:59,530
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't need to be a monk for that.

380
00:35:59,891 --> 00:36:09,436
[SPEAKER_03]: Many people nowadays will have plenty of opportunity to experiment with these things and then see if your mind remains as calm and composed as you think it now is.

381
00:36:09,476 --> 00:36:10,437
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you get to see

382
00:36:11,860 --> 00:36:13,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Refine that principle, okay?

383
00:36:13,521 --> 00:36:15,221
[SPEAKER_01]: That now there's greed, aversion, and delusion.

384
00:36:15,841 --> 00:36:22,043
[SPEAKER_01]: And you would get to see that on a more subtle level, if you act out of it, it's going to be for your harm.

385
00:36:22,123 --> 00:36:25,504
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you have a direct criteria to see that that is unwholesome.

386
00:36:25,924 --> 00:36:26,104
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

387
00:36:26,384 --> 00:36:29,665
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you also see that the Buddha said that, that that's the case.

388
00:36:29,765 --> 00:36:32,725
[SPEAKER_01]: But now you have some amount of direct confirmation.

389
00:36:32,986 --> 00:36:33,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

390
00:36:33,526 --> 00:36:34,426
[SPEAKER_01]: That it's not just a

391
00:36:34,846 --> 00:36:38,168
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is how the doubt goes, right?

392
00:36:38,789 --> 00:36:38,989
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

393
00:36:39,809 --> 00:36:50,416
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, it's... even for people who are practicing, as it says there, there's the acquisition of a view after consideration or the...

394
00:36:52,177 --> 00:37:05,286
[SPEAKER_01]: It's easy to then, when you get to a certain point in the practice, to start confusing the fact that things make sense with understanding.

395
00:37:05,966 --> 00:37:09,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, it becomes sort of that objective.

396
00:37:09,909 --> 00:37:13,231
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already quite a quite a lofty goal, by the way.

397
00:37:13,251 --> 00:37:13,671
[SPEAKER_01]: That's very rare.

398
00:37:13,691 --> 00:37:16,233
[SPEAKER_01]: But even if you get to that point, but you still don't...

399
00:37:27,019 --> 00:37:28,180
[SPEAKER_01]: See it on that internal level.

400
00:37:28,260 --> 00:37:31,201
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's literally not the dhamma.

401
00:37:31,301 --> 00:37:33,662
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if the information is the same.

402
00:37:33,822 --> 00:37:38,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it says like the dhamma can only be known for themselves by the wise.

403
00:37:38,305 --> 00:37:41,546
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, this is why it seems to me that the dhamma cannot be a system.

404
00:37:42,006 --> 00:37:48,089
[SPEAKER_03]: Because a system, even if it is fully internally coherent, is always something separate from you.

405
00:37:48,109 --> 00:37:49,050
[SPEAKER_03]: So, most people

406
00:37:50,678 --> 00:37:53,540
[SPEAKER_03]: They will be doing things that are also contradictory.

407
00:37:53,880 --> 00:37:59,603
[SPEAKER_03]: But even if those contradictions are resolved, and you say that's the system, objective, independently, then no.

408
00:38:00,263 --> 00:38:03,645
[SPEAKER_03]: It has to be... You need to become involved.

409
00:38:04,165 --> 00:38:08,188
[SPEAKER_03]: And the moment you become involved in the system, you can never see the totality of it.

410
00:38:08,208 --> 00:38:10,069
[SPEAKER_03]: Because now, in a sense, you are in it.

411
00:38:10,129 --> 00:38:12,870
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you have to discern from within that mind state.

412
00:38:13,290 --> 00:38:15,612
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it cannot be objective system.

413
00:38:16,632 --> 00:38:19,494
[SPEAKER_01]: The dhamma always applies to you.

414
00:38:19,634 --> 00:38:26,619
[SPEAKER_01]: It's never an external collection of information.

415
00:38:26,699 --> 00:38:27,599
[SPEAKER_01]: The dhamma is about you.

416
00:38:28,180 --> 00:38:29,140
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like a mirror.

417
00:38:29,320 --> 00:38:37,806
[SPEAKER_03]: However coherent or logical it might be, which is better than being incoherent and illogical, but that is not the final criteria.

418
00:38:37,846 --> 00:38:42,689
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a mirror that you're sort of examining its properties.

419
00:38:43,990 --> 00:38:48,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereas the actual purpose of the mirror is supposed to show you, like, yourself.

420
00:38:48,653 --> 00:38:48,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

421
00:38:48,993 --> 00:38:59,499
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're here occupied with, like, yeah, it's three centimeters thick and it's... that's basically when you're relating to the dhamma on that level of information.

422
00:38:59,639 --> 00:38:59,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

423
00:39:02,941 --> 00:39:04,582
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I wanted to ask as well, like,

424
00:39:08,432 --> 00:39:20,116
[SPEAKER_02]: Like in the part of the of the Sutta, the Buddha says, like, so, acting with a mind of, like, greed, aversion or delusion is something unwholesome.

425
00:39:20,536 --> 00:39:25,158
[SPEAKER_02]: And then it goes, like, after the part after, he says, like, acting with a greed of non-

426
00:39:28,147 --> 00:39:38,156
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't say, like, acting with a mind of love, acting with a mind of, like, whatever, peace or joy or whatever.

427
00:39:38,897 --> 00:39:44,842
[SPEAKER_02]: And I wanted to ask, like, why is that so and how is... why is that important for someone to...

428
00:39:47,948 --> 00:39:51,972
[SPEAKER_02]: I understand that, because if you don't, like, he cannot bring himself to that.

429
00:39:52,052 --> 00:39:54,955
[SPEAKER_02]: He still sees that as something external to himself.

430
00:39:55,135 --> 00:39:55,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

431
00:39:56,056 --> 00:40:06,666
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the thing is, whenever you approach it through that angle of cultivating positive qualities without having abandoned the negative qualities first,

432
00:40:07,707 --> 00:40:09,067
[SPEAKER_01]: It always becomes like a cover-up.

433
00:40:09,488 --> 00:40:26,193
[SPEAKER_01]: It always becomes something that's actually like... you don't want to acknowledge the fact that there would be greed, if you don't actively fabricate non-greed or renunciation, let's say.

434
00:40:26,693 --> 00:40:31,215
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to acknowledge the fact that there would be aversion, if you don't actively fabricate love.

435
00:40:31,995 --> 00:40:36,637
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for as long as a person thinks that they need to

436
00:40:37,958 --> 00:40:41,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Approach these things as activities, as things that they do.

437
00:40:42,520 --> 00:40:48,603
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't see that actually those things would arise by themselves, if they abandon the greed, aversion and delusion.

438
00:40:49,264 --> 00:40:55,206
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't see, as that sutta says, like that the defilements are not inherent to the mind.

439
00:40:55,507 --> 00:41:01,069
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like they come sort of gratuitously.

440
00:41:01,850 --> 00:41:06,732
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you remove those defilements, like if you kind of, you have a light bulb,

441
00:41:07,989 --> 00:41:10,130
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to, like, make it shine.

442
00:41:12,232 --> 00:41:18,016
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's, like, if it's not shining because it's covered by something, you don't fix it by making it shine more.

443
00:41:19,196 --> 00:41:21,538
[SPEAKER_01]: You fix it by actually removing what's obstructing it.

444
00:41:21,858 --> 00:41:24,159
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the light bulb is just how it's supposed to be.

445
00:41:24,960 --> 00:41:30,343
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why the Buddha said, if you don't don't understand that principle, he said, there is no development of the mind.

446
00:41:30,363 --> 00:41:33,205
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just, they're just covering up of one thing with another.

447
00:41:33,566 --> 00:41:34,686
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, the the

448
00:41:37,468 --> 00:41:46,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Development of whatever active qualities like renunciation, love and wisdom.

449
00:41:47,142 --> 00:41:51,768
[SPEAKER_01]: If you think that's what it's saying, when in reality it's saying non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion,

450
00:41:54,091 --> 00:41:59,653
[SPEAKER_01]: That would always be dependent on, as we said, these external dimensions.

451
00:41:59,673 --> 00:42:08,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, somebody told you how to cultivate pronunciation, love, or whatever.

452
00:42:08,715 --> 00:42:09,776
[SPEAKER_01]: You didn't see it for yourself.

453
00:42:10,976 --> 00:42:11,736
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when it would be.

454
00:42:11,896 --> 00:42:12,116
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

455
00:42:12,476 --> 00:42:15,777
[SPEAKER_03]: And also, there would be actually, logically, a contradiction in that.

456
00:42:15,817 --> 00:42:21,599
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, how can you gradually build up love if a version hasn't been abandoned?

457
00:42:22,455 --> 00:42:23,216
[SPEAKER_03]: That's a contradiction.

458
00:42:23,857 --> 00:42:29,523
[SPEAKER_03]: You cannot have aversion and at the same time start building up love or friendliness.

459
00:42:30,043 --> 00:42:36,751
[SPEAKER_03]: So the friendliness begins with the complete absence of the aversion.

460
00:42:36,871 --> 00:42:39,794
[SPEAKER_03]: So the whole thing is an uncovering or an undoing.

461
00:42:40,375 --> 00:42:42,837
[SPEAKER_03]: And this is also why this famous Upādāpātimokkha

462
00:42:43,558 --> 00:42:44,379
[SPEAKER_03]: Right?

463
00:42:44,440 --> 00:42:49,187
[SPEAKER_03]: Which is sometimes quoted as like a summary of what the Buddha teaches.

464
00:42:49,207 --> 00:42:53,074
[SPEAKER_03]: The first line is that he refrains from doing something unwholesome.

465
00:42:53,863 --> 00:42:56,565
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, he develops what is wholesome.

466
00:42:56,725 --> 00:43:02,188
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't start by saying you develop what is wholesome, and then, afterwards, you could consider whether you also undo what is unwholesome.

467
00:43:02,348 --> 00:43:02,989
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't say that.

468
00:43:03,029 --> 00:43:03,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

469
00:43:03,389 --> 00:43:05,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, that's the order of the four-way strivings.

470
00:43:05,970 --> 00:43:06,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

471
00:43:06,391 --> 00:43:12,774
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always abandoning, preventing the restraining, unarisen unwholesome states from arising.

472
00:43:12,854 --> 00:43:13,055
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

473
00:43:13,175 --> 00:43:14,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Abandoning arisen unwholesome states.

474
00:43:15,136 --> 00:43:15,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

475
00:43:15,376 --> 00:43:18,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you're in the position to cultivate unarisen unwholesome states.

476
00:43:18,298 --> 00:43:18,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

477
00:43:18,778 --> 00:43:21,740
[SPEAKER_01]: And, finally, you have to protect the arisen wholesome states.

478
00:43:21,760 --> 00:43:22,100
[SPEAKER_01]: That's right.

479
00:43:22,340 --> 00:43:22,680
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

480
00:43:24,649 --> 00:43:31,900
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the main issue with that, as well as the aspect of timelessness that we mentioned.

481
00:43:32,100 --> 00:43:34,143
[SPEAKER_01]: The dhamma is supposed to be directly visible.

482
00:43:34,584 --> 00:43:38,009
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what happens if somebody can't get themselves to feel love?

483
00:43:39,648 --> 00:43:49,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, like, the benefit of the dhamma wouldn't be directly visible, because it takes time to change your mind, to force it to have these states.

484
00:43:50,436 --> 00:43:54,598
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it's about non-greed, well, that's always presently possible.

485
00:43:54,719 --> 00:43:58,881
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about not making a choice of greed, not making a choice of aversion.

486
00:43:59,462 --> 00:44:00,803
[SPEAKER_01]: That's always directly available.

487
00:44:01,282 --> 00:44:11,966
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, and even when the mind is affected by greed, to not act out by body-speech, or as we discussed earlier on the mental level, right, that is already how there is that purity.

488
00:44:12,246 --> 00:44:21,049
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then the mind can still be affected by, let's say, sensuality, but there would be purity as long as there's no acting out by body-speech and mind.

489
00:44:21,149 --> 00:44:28,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and that doesn't depend on time, and it doesn't depend on you, on somebody else telling you, like, if you do this, you will end up with love later on.

490
00:44:28,351 --> 00:44:28,691
[SPEAKER_03]: That's right.

491
00:44:28,871 --> 00:44:29,812
[SPEAKER_03]: Non-acting out

492
00:44:30,732 --> 00:44:31,994
[SPEAKER_03]: Doesn't depend on time.

493
00:44:32,174 --> 00:44:34,397
[SPEAKER_03]: You just immediately don't do it.

494
00:44:34,937 --> 00:44:42,226
[SPEAKER_03]: Whereas to positively do something will always in that sense involve time, because it might take a little while before you're going to see the results.

495
00:44:42,367 --> 00:44:43,989
[SPEAKER_03]: And so that's not timeless dhamma anymore then.

496
00:44:44,029 --> 00:44:45,010
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

497
00:44:45,980 --> 00:44:56,925
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, even if it's as quick as just thinking a thought of love, it's already... even if it's like a... that quick of time is still... Yeah, and also, like, you never... you never start from that.

498
00:44:57,045 --> 00:45:04,848
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you would have started from a point of view where it took time, and you would have taken it on faith that somebody else told you, you'll end up there, which means you didn't.

499
00:45:05,608 --> 00:45:06,509
[SPEAKER_01]: You use this advice.

500
00:45:06,529 --> 00:45:16,298
[SPEAKER_01]: You just found one guy who was telling you something, and you followed that, but you never actually understood, presently, what is that non-greed, and how can I abandon it?

501
00:45:16,338 --> 00:45:20,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Somebody else told you, like, do this, and you end up with the opposite.

502
00:45:20,141 --> 00:45:23,705
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that is why, you know, there's this whole idea of applying an antidote.

503
00:45:24,085 --> 00:45:28,949
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, oh, I feel ill-will, I should practice metta as an antidote to the ill-will.

504
00:45:29,690 --> 00:45:32,332
[SPEAKER_03]: But that's a misunderstanding.

505
00:45:32,932 --> 00:45:39,577
[SPEAKER_03]: Sometimes, it seems to me, that's also sort of an import from a more, let's say, scientific or biological way of thinking.

506
00:45:39,677 --> 00:45:47,163
[SPEAKER_03]: When the body is healed, you might find herbs and medications, something external to this body, that you apply in order to heal.

507
00:45:47,643 --> 00:45:53,308
[SPEAKER_03]: So, on the material level, there is something to say for this whole logic of applying antidotes.

508
00:45:53,348 --> 00:45:54,308
[SPEAKER_03]: You apply the antidote?

509
00:45:54,589 --> 00:45:59,152
[SPEAKER_03]: But you cannot take that and then apply it to the level of the mind, because that's a confusion.

510
00:45:59,232 --> 00:46:07,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, no, but even on the level of the body, you apply this medicine, but did you understand what the source of the problem was in the first place, or did the pain just go away?

511
00:46:08,555 --> 00:46:10,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

512
00:46:10,996 --> 00:46:19,719
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the thing, like, people can succeed in developing love or whatever, but if you ask them, what was that ill will that you abandoned in the first place?

513
00:46:19,859 --> 00:46:22,380
[SPEAKER_01]: Was it just unpleasant feelings that came up by themselves?

514
00:46:22,800 --> 00:46:25,121
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you look closely, most of the time, that's the implication.

515
00:46:25,741 --> 00:46:28,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Ill will was just the fact that I didn't feel

516
00:46:29,262 --> 00:46:30,583
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not the way I feel now.

517
00:46:31,884 --> 00:46:34,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereas that's... that's not what a will is.

518
00:46:34,887 --> 00:46:36,288
[SPEAKER_01]: A will is not unpleasant feeling.

519
00:46:37,028 --> 00:46:39,551
[SPEAKER_01]: A will is a choice that you make on top of unpleasant feeling.

520
00:46:39,571 --> 00:46:47,237
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't understand that, well, again, the only way you'll be able to make progress is through some external information.

521
